activities:
  # Day -2: Early groundwork
  - type: note
    priority_ref: active
    title: "Climate financing proposal - initial review"
    created: "-2d"
    tags:
      pinned: [user]
    notes:
      - created: "-2d 09:00"
        content: |
          **CONFIDENTIAL - State Department Cable**
          **From: Regional Climate Office**
          **Subject: Multilateral Climate Finance Framework - Draft Proposal**

          Ambassador Wyler,

          Per your request, draft proposal for climate financing mechanism. Summary:

          **Objective:** $50B multilateral fund for climate adaptation in developing nations
          **Structure:** Consortium of US, EU, UK, select Asian partners
          **Challenge:** US Congress skeptical of "blank check commitments"

          **Current language (problematic):**
          "Binding commitment to contribute proportional share based on GDP..."

          This will die in committee. Need to reframe.

          Thoughts?

          - Regional Climate Office
        source:
          type: diplomatic_cable
          title: Climate Finance Draft
          href: mailto:climate@state.gov

  - type: action
    priority_ref: policy
    title: "Reframe climate proposal language - make it safe"
    assignee_ref: user
    on: "-2d"
    tags:
      urgent: [user]
    notes:
      - created: "-2d 10:30"
        content: |
          The proposal is good policy. The language will kill it.

          **Problem:** "Binding commitment" = political suicide
          **Solution:** Reframe as "leadership opportunity" with "flexible participation framework"

          Congress doesn't want to be bound. They want to lead. Give them the narrative they need.

          Start with the economic opportunity: green tech exports, job creation, global influence.
          Then slide in the funding mechanism as "strategic investment."

          Same outcome. Different story. That's the work.

  - type: event
    priority_ref: strategic
    title: "Stuart - pre-meeting alignment call"
    at: "-2d 14:00 / -2d 14:25"
    notes:
      - author_ref: stuart
        created: "-2d 14:05"
        content: |
          Kate - on board with the climate proposal but Treasury will want guarantees on ROI. Climate adaptation doesn't have an ROI column.
        source:
          type: signal_call
          title: "Stuart Hayford - Signal"
      - author_ref: user
        created: "-2d 14:10"
        content: |
          Then we give them a different spreadsheet. Infrastructure development. Trade relationships. Strategic positioning in developing markets.
        source:
          type: signal_call
          title: "Stuart Hayford - Signal"
      - author_ref: stuart
        created: "-2d 14:12"
        content: |
          So we lie?
        source:
          type: signal_call
          title: "Stuart Hayford - Signal"
      - author_ref: user
        created: "-2d 14:13"
        content: |
          We emphasize different truths.
        source:
          type: signal_call
          title: "Stuart Hayford - Signal"
      - author_ref: stuart
        created: "-2d 14:25"
        content: |
          Fair enough. I'll help sell it to Treasury using the economic angle. Meeting next week with EU partners - I'll pre-position the economic framing with UK delegation.
        source:
          type: signal_call
          title: "Stuart Hayford - Signal"
    tags:
      pinned: [user]

  - type: note
    priority_ref: household
    title: "Hal - scheduling conflict"
    created: "-2d"
    notes:
      - created: "-2d 16:00"
        content: |
          **Signal message - Hal**

          "Paris conference moved to same week as your Berlin trip. Can we make it work or do I need to push Paris?"

          ---

          **Me:** "How important is Paris?"

          **Hal:** "Very. But Berlin is your heavy lift."

          **Me:** "I'll ask Eidra to cover the Paris opener. I'll join by video for the critical session."

          **Hal:** "That works. Miss you."

          **Me:** "Miss you too. Video call tonight?"

          **Hal:** "I'm in Jakarta. Different tonight. Tomorrow your morning?"

          **Me:** "Perfect. 7am my time."

          Married to someone with an equally impossible schedule. We make it work. Barely.
        source:
          type: secure_message
          title: Signal - Hal
        mentions:
          - hal
          - eidra
    tags:
      pinned: [user]

  - type: action
    priority_ref: strategic
    title: "Prep Veronica (EU) for climate proposal"
    assignee_ref: user
    on: "-2d"
    notes:
      - created: "-2d 17:00"
        content: |
          Veronica (EU Climate Envoy) is key ally. Need to brief her before the formal proposal goes wide.

          She'll help frame it for EU member states. But she needs to believe:
          1. US is serious (not just posturing)
          2. Framework is achievable (not aspirational)
          3. She gets credit (not just US leadership narrative)

          Will set up call for tomorrow. Casual, exploratory. Plant the seeds before the official ask.
        mentions:
          - ronnie

  # Day -1: Shaping the narrative
  - type: event
    priority_ref: strategic
    title: "Veronica Penn - exploratory call"
    at: "-1d 10:00 / -1d 10:45"
    notes:
      - created: "-1d 11:00"
        content: |
          **Video call with Veronica Penn (EU Climate Envoy)**

          Walked her through the proposal framework. Emphasized:
          - US commitment (with congressional buy-in strategy)
          - EU leadership role (not US dominance)
          - Practical timeline (pilot phase before full rollout)

          **Veronica:** "This sounds more modest than your usual proposals. Is that tactical?"

          **Me:** "It's a pilot that threatens to succeed. Easier to expand a success than launch a moonshot."

          **Veronica:** "Clever. I can sell a pilot."

          She'll bring it to EU Climate Council next week. Framed as "EU-US collaborative framework."

          Perfect. Let them think it's their idea.
        mentions:
          - ronnie
    tags:
      star: [user]

  - type: note
    priority_ref: active
    title: "Dennison update - White House concerns"
    created: "-1d"
    notes:
      - created: "-1d 13:00"
        content: |
          **From: William Dennison (Secretary of State)**
          **Subject: RE: Climate Finance Proposal**

          Kate,

          White House is nervous about new multilateral commitments. Midterms coming up. Every spending proposal is getting extra scrutiny.

          Can you reframe this as deficit-neutral? Or at least revenue-adjacent?

          I know you'll figure it out. You always do.

          - Dennison
        source:
          type: email
          title: White House Concerns
          href: mailto:william.dennison@state.gov
        mentions:
          - dennison
    tags:
      warning: [user]

  - type: action
    priority_ref: policy
    title: "Add revenue angle to climate proposal"
    assignee_ref: user
    on: "-1d"
    tags:
      urgent: [user]
    notes:
      - created: "-1d 14:00"
        content: |
          **@plot, suggest revenue-generating mechanisms that could offset climate fund contributions**
        ai_query: true
      - created: "-1d 14:00"
        content: |
          **Plot AI Response:**

          Potential revenue/offset mechanisms for climate finance proposal:

          1. **Green technology export credits** - Frame US contributions as investments in domestic green tech that gets exported through the fund
          2. **Carbon border adjustment revenues** - Tie fund contributions to future carbon import fees (already under discussion)
          3. **Public-private partnership structure** - Leverage private capital through federal guarantees (reduces direct budget impact)
          4. **Repayment structure for middle-income recipients** - Not grants, but low-interest loans with long horizons

          Recommendation: Emphasize #3 (PPP structure). Plays well with both parties, reduces fiscal exposure, maintains commitment.
      - created: "-1d 14:15"
        content: |
          ---

          Good. The PPP angle works.

          "US leadership through strategic investment partnerships" - contributes less direct federal money, leverages private sector, achieves the same climate outcomes.

          Congress likes private sector involvement. Treasury likes reduced budget exposure. We get the climate fund.

          Different story. Same outcome.

  - type: event
    priority_ref: household
    title: "Video call with Hal"
    at: "-1d 19:00 / -1d 19:35"
    notes:
      - created: "-1d 19:45"
        content: |
          **Video call from hotel room**

          Caught up on life outside work (there isn't much, but we try).

          **Hal:** "How's the climate thing going?"

          **Me:** "Turning a $50 billion ask into a public-private partnership that Congress can't say no to."

          **Hal:** "So, normal Wednesday."

          **Me:** "How's Jakarta?"

          **Hal:** "Hot. Complicated. The usual."

          We talked for 30 minutes. Didn't solve anything. Just... connected.

          **Hal:** "I miss your face in person."

          **Me:** "Two weeks until we're in the same city."

          **Hal:** "Counting down."

          Me too.
        mentions:
          - hal
    tags:
      star: [user]

  - type: note
    priority_ref: strategic
    title: "Billie - ground-level intelligence"
    created: "-1d"
    notes:
      - created: "-1d 15:00"
        content: |
          **Signal message - Billie Appiah (UK Embassy Staff)**

          "Kate, heads up: French delegation is drafting their own climate proposal. Heard it might conflict with yours. Want me to find out more?"

          ---

          **Me:** "Yes. Quietly. Let me know what they're planning."

          Of course France has their own proposal. They always do.

          Need to know if it's competitive or complementary. If competitive, need to neutralize it. If complementary, need to merge them and let France think they won.
        source:
          type: secure_message
          title: Signal - Billie
        mentions:
          - billie

  - type: event
    priority_ref: active
    title: "Austin Ganon - congressional liaison update"
    at: "-1d 16:00 / -1d 16:20"
    notes:
      - created: "-1d 16:30"
        content: |
          **Call with Austin Ganon (State Dept Congressional Affairs)**

          Austin's been testing the climate proposal with key congressional offices.

          **Results:**
          - Progressive wing: Love the climate ambition, skeptical of private sector involvement
          - Moderate wing: Like the PPP structure, want more budget neutrality guarantees
          - Opposition: Will oppose anything multilateral (unchangeable)

          **Austin:** "You need to pick your coalition. Can't please everyone."

          **Me:** "Moderates. Build from the center. Progressives will come along when they see it's the only viable path."

          **Austin:** "That's cold."

          **Me:** "That's counting votes."

          He'll set up briefings with moderate leadership next week.
        mentions:
          - ganon
    tags:
      decision: [user]

  # Day 0 (Base date): The pivot and the proposal
  - type: note
    priority_ref: active
    title: "CLIMATE FINANCE PROPOSAL - Final draft (version 4.0)"
    created: "-1d"
    tags:
      pinned: [user]
      urgent: [user]
      decision: [user]
    notes:
      - created: "-1d 18:00"
        content: |
          ## Global Climate Resilience Partnership (GCRP)
          ### Proposal - Version 4.0 (FINAL)

          **Original concept:** Multilateral climate fund, $50B, binding commitments
          **Final concept:** Public-private partnership for climate resilience infrastructure

          ---

          ### Executive Summary

          The Global Climate Resilience Partnership represents a **strategic investment framework** that positions the United States as the leading partner in climate adaptation infrastructure while **leveraging private capital** to minimize federal budget exposure.

          *(Translation: Same climate fund. Different story.)*

          ---

          ### Structure

          **Total capitalization:** $50B over 10 years
          **US contribution:** $8B federal (primary catalyst)
          **Leveraged private capital:** $25B (through federal guarantees)
          **Partner contributions:** $17B (EU, UK, Asian partners)

          **Federal exposure:** $8B direct + $25B in guarantees (with <5% projected draw rate based on historical PPP performance)

          *(Translation: We're asking for $8B in direct spending, not $50B. The rest is guarantees and other people's money.)*

          ---

          ### Revenue Model

          1. **Green technology export platform:** US companies get preferential access to fund-supported projects (export revenue)
          2. **Repayment structure:** Middle-income recipients repay low-interest loans over 30 years
          3. **Carbon credit generation:** Projects generate carbon credits that can be sold in compliance markets
          4. **Private sector returns:** Private investors receive modest returns on infrastructure investments

          *(Translation: This isn't aid. This is strategic economic positioning that happens to address climate change.)*

          ---

          ### Governance

          - **Board:** US, EU, UK, 2 rotating partner seats
          - **US voting share:** 30% (proportional to contribution + guarantee exposure)
          - **Decision threshold:** 60% (ensures US veto on major decisions)

          *(Translation: Multilateral in name, US-led in practice.)*

          ---

          ### Timeline

          **Phase 1 (Year 1-2):** Pilot program - $5B capitalization, 3 regions, proof of concept
          **Phase 2 (Year 3-5):** Expansion based on Phase 1 performance
          **Phase 3 (Year 6-10):** Full deployment (contingent on congressional reauthorization)

          *(Translation: A modest pilot that threatens to succeed. Easier to kill a success than stop a moonshot, but harder to justify killing it.)*

          ---

          ### Political Framing

          **For progressives:** "Largest US climate commitment in history"
          **For moderates:** "Fiscally responsible, private-sector leveraged infrastructure investment"
          **For skeptics:** "Strategic positioning in developing markets, export opportunities"

          All true. Different emphasis.

          ---

          ### Revision History

          **V1.0:** Binding multilateral commitment - DOA in Congress
          **V2.0:** Added flexible framework language - better, still too ambitious
          **V3.0:** Introduced PPP structure - getting closer
          **V4.0:** Full revenue model + phased timeline - this is the one

          ---

          ### What Changed

          - "Commitment" → "Partnership"
          - "Contribution" → "Investment"
          - "Climate aid" → "Resilience infrastructure"
          - "$50B ask" → "$8B catalyst"
          - "Permanent fund" → "Pilot program with expansion path"

          **Same outcome. Different story. That's the work.**
      - created: "-1d 18:45"
        content: |
          ---

          **@plot, identify potential opposition arguments and draft responses**
        ai_query: true
      - created: "-1d 18:45"
        content: |
          **Plot AI Response:**

          **Likely opposition arguments with recommended responses:**

          **1. "This is a blank check for climate activists"**
          Response: "It's a structured investment with clear ROI metrics, private sector oversight, and congressional reauthorization requirements."

          **2. "Why should US taxpayers fund other countries' infrastructure?"**
          Response: "US companies build the infrastructure. US technology gets exported. US workers benefit from the supply chain. This is about American economic leadership."

          **3. "This is just foreign aid with a new name"**
          Response: "Foreign aid doesn't have repayment structures or private sector returns. This is infrastructure investment that happens to address climate resilience."

          **4. "The private sector should do this without government guarantees"**
          Response: "They should, and they will - in projects with this guarantee structure proving the market. We're creating the conditions for private sector success."

          **5. "This benefits China/India more than the US"**
          Response: "Only if we let them. US participation ensures US companies, US standards, and US influence shape the next generation of global infrastructure."
      - created: "-1d 19:00"
        content: |
          ---

          Good. I'm ready to defend this.

          Briefing congressional moderates tomorrow. EU partners on Friday. Full proposal goes to White House next week.

          It'll work. It has to work. I've made it as safe as possible without gutting it.

  - type: event
    priority_ref: strategic
    title: "Eidra - proposal review and feedback"
    at: "-1d 09:00 / -1d 09:45"
    notes:
      - author_ref: eidra
        created: "-1d 09:50"
        content: |
          Kate walked me through the final draft. She's basically transformed this into something completely different - same fund, same amount, same climate outcomes, but the framing makes it passable.

          I'll present it at the Paris conference. She'll join via video for questions. She's really good at making dangerous ideas safe.
      - author_ref: user
        created: "-1d 10:00"
        content: |
          Good meeting with Eidra. She gets it - the framing is everything. Same policy, different story.

          She's better at the optimistic pitch. I'm better at the defensive maneuvering. Good division of labor.

  - type: action
    author_ref: ganon
    priority_ref: active
    title: "Prepare congressional briefing materials"
    assignee_ref: ganon
    on: "+0d"
    tags:
      urgent: [user, ganon]
    notes:
      - author_ref: ganon
        created: "+0d 10:30"
        content: |
          Preparing materials for tomorrow's congressional briefing per Kate's requirements:
          - Executive summary (2 pages max)
          - Budget impact analysis (emphasize guarantees vs direct spending)
          - Economic benefit projections (jobs, exports)
          - Comparison to alternative approaches (show this is the conservative option)
          - FAQ on common objections

          Making it easy to say yes, hard to say no.

  - type: note
    priority_ref: strategic
    title: "Billie intel - French proposal details"
    created: "-1d"
    tags:
      warning: [user]
    notes:
      - created: "-1d 11:15"
        content: |
          **Signal message - Billie**

          "French proposal is very similar to yours but emphasizes EU leadership and excludes private sector involvement. They're positioning it as 'pure climate commitment' vs 'profit-driven infrastructure.'

          They haven't seen your draft yet. Planning to present next week.

          - B"

          ---

          Perfect. They're walking into a trap they don't know exists.

          Our proposal is already socializing with EU partners. By the time France presents theirs, the narrative will be "US-EU partnership already in motion."

          France will look late to the game. They'll have to join our framework or launch a competing initiative that splits EU support.

          They'll join. And they'll claim they shaped it.

          That's fine. I don't need credit. I need the fund to exist.
        source:
          type: secure_message
          title: Signal - Billie
        mentions:
          - billie
          - alouette

  - type: event
